syntax: parse zsh case groups and braces, coproc, -N, and `}` after builtins

A handful of smaller zsh gaps, each valid zsh (zshmisc(1), zshexpn(1),
zshmisc(1) "CONDITIONAL EXPRESSIONS"):

	case $x in (a|b)*) cmd ;; esac      # a group starting the first pattern
	case $x in (#i)yes) cmd ;; esac     # globbing flags
	case $x { a) cmd ;; }               # the alternate form, like mksh's
	coproc cat                          # a coprocess, with no name
	{ local x }                         # "}" closes the block
	{ let x=1 }
	[[ -N $file ]]

Reproducer, before this change:

	$ printf 'case $x in (a|b)*) cmd ;; esac\n' | shfmt -ln zsh
	<standard input>:1:17: a command can only contain words and redirects; encountered `)`
	$ printf 'case $x { a) cmd ;; }\n' | shfmt -ln zsh
	<standard input>:1:1: `case i {` is a mksh feature; tried parsing as zsh
	$ printf '() { local x }\n' | shfmt -ln zsh
	<standard input>:1:4: reached EOF without matching `{` with `}`
	$ printf '[[ -N $f ]]\n' | shfmt -ln zsh
	<standard input>:1:7: not a valid test operator: `$`

and `coproc cat` parsed as a call to a command named coproc.

Zsh tells a leading "(" which opens a case item's pattern list apart from
one which starts a group in its first pattern by what follows the
matching ")": a blank or separator for the former. The parser now does the
same, and builds a group as literal parts of its pattern word. The printer
adds the optional "(" when the first of several patterns starts with a
group, which would otherwise read as the opening one.

Part of https://github.com/mvdan/sh/issues/120.

